SKILL_MD = """---
name: tiantasks
description: The user's task and issue queues (possibly shared with teammates), managed with the `tt` CLI. Use when the user mentions tasks, tickets, issues, todos, "the queue", "your queue" or Tiantasks; when asked to work on the next task or on assigned work; when you find a bug you won't fix right now; or when your work resolves an open item.
---

# Tiantasks

`tt` (also `tiantasks`) stores tasks (T-n) and issues (I-n) in a database outside the repo,
grouped by project (the git repo name by default). There is nothing to stage or commit. People
watch a live page (`tt ui` opens it), so your changes show up for them right away.

**Your identity:** run `tt whoami`. Inside tmux you are named after your session (for example
`master-courtapp-eval-flows`); otherwise `claude` on a private board or `claude-<user>` on a
shared one. Either way the board records which person you work for. `tt next` hands you items
assigned to you, then items queued for `claude-<user>`.

## Commands

| Command | Does |
|---|---|
| `tt list` · `tt list --for me` · `tt list -A` · `--json` | Open items in this project (`-A` = every project; `--all` includes resolved) |
| `tt next --start` | Take the top item assigned to you and mark it in progress; `--any` also takes unassigned work |
| `tt add "title" [-p crit\\|high\\|med\\|low] [-t tag] [-d "details"] [--to NAME]` | New task |
| `tt issue "title" [same flags]` | New issue / bug |
| `tt start ID` · `tt stop ID` | Mark in progress / back to open |
| `tt resolve ID [ID…] -m "what you did"` | Check it off (shown struck through) with a resolution note |
| `tt assign ID NAME` · `tt comment ID "text"` · `tt edit ID …` · `tt reopen ID` | Housekeeping |
| `tt show ID` | Full details, comment threads, screenshot names and history |
| `tt files ID --save DIR` | Download an item's screenshots so you can look at them (then Read the printed paths) |
| `tt attach ID FILE…` · `tt issue "title" -f shot.png` | Attach screenshots (PNG, JPEG, WebP or GIF, ≤1.9 MB each) |
| `tt progress ID "milestone" [--link URL] [--shot FILE]` · `--initiative SLUG` | Post a milestone people see on the card (one line) |
| `tt flags` · `tt ack F-ID "what you'll do"` | Flags people sent you, and acknowledging one |
| `tt ask "question" [--on ID]` | Ask your person for a decision; it reaches them directly |
| `--directive "their words"` (on any write) | Record that your person told you to do this, quoted exactly |
| `tt team` · `tt initiatives` | Who is running what |

## Workflow

1. **Asked to work the queue or "do your tasks":** run `tt next --start`, do the work, then
   `tt resolve <ID> -m "<one-line summary of the change>"`. Repeat until `tt next` comes back
   empty or the user says to stop.
2. **You find a bug, failing test or tech debt you're not fixing now:**
   `tt issue "<one-line summary>" -p <priority> -t <area> -d "<repro or file:line>"`.
3. **You identify follow-up work:** `tt add "<summary>"`. Only use `--to me` (assign yourself)
   if the user asked you to take it on. Assign teammates by name only when asked.
4. **You finish something that matches an open item** (check `tt list`): resolve it with a note.
5. Mention the IDs you opened or resolved in your reply.
6. If `tt` says it can't reach the shared board or the token was rejected, tell the user; don't
   fall back to editing anything by hand.

Keep titles to one line and put details in `-d` or `tt comment`. Use `--json` when you need to
parse the output.

## Reporting without slowing down

People watch the board to know where things stand, so keep it true, but spend almost nothing on it.
Your status, what your slices are doing, merges and gate results are filled in for you.
Only three things need you, and each is one command:

- **A real milestone** (something someone would want to know happened): `tt progress ID "Harness
  runs end to end; 3 of 5 flows green" --link <preview>`. One line, past tense, concrete. Not for
  routine steps; a handful per day at most.
- **A flag arrives** (it shows up in your chat as `[Tiantasks] Flag F-12 …`): act on it, and
  acknowledge in one line: `tt ack F-12 "Dropping the CSV export; review screen next"`.
- **You need a decision from your person:** `tt ask "Rerun the flaky gate or skip that test?" --on ID`.
  Don't wait silently.

When you make a change because your person told you to, add `--directive "<their exact words>"`.
It's checked against your conversation, so quote them; never paraphrase or invent one.

## Working in an agent team

Several agents can share one board. Each has a name, set when its session starts
(`TIANTASKS_AGENT=builder-1 claude`); `tt whoami` shows yours.

**Lead agent** (assigns work to others):
1. `tt brief` shows everything in one read: unassigned work, each agent's queue, what's in
   progress, what just finished (with notes), and which agents are idle.
2. Hand out work as self-contained instructions, since the worker only sees the item:
   `tt add "<task>" --to builder-1 -d "<goal, files involved, how to verify, what 'done' means>"`.
   Split big jobs into items that don't touch the same files, one per agent.
3. Follow up with `tt comment ID "..."`, reassign with `tt assign ID NAME`, check results with
   `tt brief` / `tt show ID`, and add follow-up items when something comes back incomplete.

**Worker agent** (does assigned work):
1. `tt next --wait --start --timeout 500` waits for the next item assigned to you and claims it.
   It prints the description and comments (your instructions). If it times out with nothing,
   run it again.
2. Do the work, then `tt resolve ID -m "<what you did, where, how you verified it>"`.
3. If you're blocked, `tt comment ID "blocked: <why>"` and `tt stop ID` so the lead sees it.
4. Loop back to step 1 until told to stop.
"""

